WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.144
A sufficient assumption, added to the premises, establishes the conclusion.

2
00:00:05.444 --> 00:00:09.967
It can be stronger than necessary as long as it actually completes the argument.

3
00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.528
Every sponsored exhibit is registered.

4
00:00:12.828 --> 00:00:16.199
The author concludes that every sponsored exhibit is insured.

5
00:00:16.499 --> 00:00:18.291
The premise reaches registered.

6
00:00:18.591 --> 00:00:23.497
Registered → insured completes the path from sponsorship to insurance.

7
00:00:23.797 --> 00:00:26.123
Insured → registered does not.

8
00:00:26.423 --> 00:00:35.575
Sponsorship alone was not asserted as an actual exhibit fact; the conclusion is a general rule, so the completed proof should retain that form.

9
00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:40.035
The introductory bridge exercise now becomes an argument test.

10
00:00:40.335 --> 00:00:48.292
An answer qualifies because the premises plus that answer force the exact conclusion, not because it is a reasonable policy.

11
00:00:48.592 --> 00:00:51.792
Do not put the proposed conclusion among the assumed rules.

12
00:00:52.092 --> 00:00:54.716
Identify the conditions its proof would need.

13
00:00:55.016 --> 00:00:57.256
Check the strongest part of the conclusion.

14
00:00:57.556 --> 00:01:02.868
An assumption that reaches only one member of a required conjunction is not sufficient.

15
00:01:03.168 --> 00:01:09.653
A conclusion about a whole class also cannot be proved by an addition covering only one named case.
